"""Full manuscript for Tech for Everyone — 10 chapters.
Each chapter: (title, [paragraphs]). Sample uses ch.1-3 trimmed; full uses all."""

CHAPTERS = [
    ("Thinking in Systems", [
        "Technology feels overwhelming mostly because people memorize steps instead of understanding systems. A step, click here, then here, works until an update moves the button, and then the memorized knowledge evaporates. A system-level understanding, what a computer is for, how data flows between places, why software behaves as it does, survives every interface change. This book teaches the system level, because that layer has remained stable for decades while screens and buttons churn constantly.",
        "Nearly everything you do digitally follows one pattern: data is created somewhere, stored somewhere, moved across networks, processed, and displayed. Take sending a message. Your words become data on your device, travel through networks to a server, get stored there, then get delivered to your friend's device and displayed. Every app you use, photos, banking, maps, games, is a variation on this same loop. Once you see the loop, new apps stop being mysteries; they are just different costumes on a familiar skeleton.",
        "The second foundational idea is abstraction, the practice of using tools without needing to know their insides. You already do this with your car: you drive without understanding fuel injection. Technology works identically. You need to know what a cloud service does with your files, not the engineering of its data centers. Professionals stack abstractions all day; the skill worth developing is knowing which layer matters for your current question, so small problems stay small.",
        "Systems thinking also explains why technology fails in patterns rather than at random. When something breaks, the fault lives somewhere along a chain: your device, its software, your network, the remote service, or the account connecting them. Troubleshooting is simply testing each link in order, which Chapter 8 turns into a full method. People who feel powerless around computers usually believe failures are chaotic; people who fix things calmly see failure as a chain with a broken link to find.",
        "Feedback loops shape how these systems behave over time. Recommendation feeds learn from every pause and click, showing more of whatever held your attention, which can quietly narrow into compulsive use. Storage fills gradually until one day it is full. Passwords age until one leaks. Understanding that digital systems continuously respond to your behavior lets you intervene deliberately, adjusting inputs before outputs become problems, the same way you steer a car rather than merely riding it.",
        "A practical exercise cements this chapter: pick any app you use daily and narrate its data journey aloud. Where is the information created, where probably stored, who can see it, what happens if the network drops? You will not answer perfectly, and precision is not the point. The habit of asking structural questions instead of memorizing procedures is the entire foundation this book builds on.",
    ]),
    ("Staying Safe Online", [
        "Online safety rests on three habits that prevent the vast majority of real-world harm: strong unique passwords held by a password manager, two-factor authentication on important accounts, and healthy suspicion of unexpected messages. Everything else in digital security elaborates these three. Master them and you are safer than most professionals; skip them and no antivirus subscription compensates.",
        "Passwords fail when reused because attackers automate: one breached site's password list gets tried against email, banking, and social accounts within minutes. Unique passwords contain this blast radius, but humans cannot remember fifty unique random strings, which is precisely the problem password managers solve. They generate and store unguessable passwords behind one master passphrase you actually remember. The adjustment takes an afternoon, and the master passphrase deserves length over complexity: four unrelated words beat eight characters of symbols.",
        "Two-factor authentication adds a second lock, typically a code from an app or a physical key, so a stolen password alone cannot open your account. Enable it first on email, because email is the master key to everything else; anyone controlling your inbox can reset your other passwords at will. Authentication apps offer stronger protection than text messages, though texts still defeat most attacks. Five minutes per account, once, permanently raises the wall around your life.",
        "Phishing remains the top threat because it attacks psychology rather than code. Modern phishing emails mimic invoices, delivery notices, security alerts, and boss requests with convincing polish. The defense is procedural, not perceptual: never click links in unexpected urgent messages. Instead, navigate independently, typing the company's address yourself or opening the official app, and check there. Urgency is the tell; legitimate organizations rarely demand action within hours and threaten consequences for hesitation.",
        "Software updates deserve reframing from nagging chores into free security labor performed for you. Most updates patch specific holes attackers already exploit, so delayed updates leave documented doors open. Turn on automatic updates everywhere, operating system, browser, phone, router if possible, and reboot when asked. Similarly, review app permissions occasionally: a flashlight app requesting contacts and location deserves deletion, since permissions granted casually accumulate into surveillance nobody intended.",
        "Public networks and public devices require modest extra care. On shared Wi-Fi in cafes or airports, prefer sites and apps using HTTPS, indicated by the padlock, and avoid signing into sensitive accounts unless necessary; modern encryption covers most casual risk. Public computers deserve deeper suspicion: assume keyloggers, never access financial or primary email accounts, and always log out fully. These cautions take seconds and eliminate scenarios that otherwise ruin weeks.",
        "Finally, plan for compromise before it happens, because recovery speed matters more than perfection. Know today how to reach each critical account's recovery options, keep backup codes offline on paper, and list which accounts matter most. If something goes wrong, change passwords from a clean device, check account activity logs for intruders, and alert contacts if messages went out in your name. People with plans recover in hours; people without them spend weeks untangling identity theft that early action could have contained.",
    ]),
    ("Understanding Your Devices", [
        "Your devices differ in form but share the same anatomy: hardware doing physical work, an operating system managing that hardware, applications running on top, and your data living underneath. Phones, laptops, tablets, and even smart televisions follow this layered model. Learning the anatomy once transfers everywhere, because every device question becomes locatable: is this about the hardware, the system, an app, or my files?",
        "Storage confuses people more than necessary because two different kinds hide behind one word. Memory, called RAM, is temporary workspace: open apps occupy it, and closing them frees it. Storage, the drive, is permanent filing space holding your files, apps, and system itself. Slow devices usually lack available RAM, meaning too many things run simultaneously; 'storage almost full' warnings concern the permanent kind. Knowing which complaint you have points directly at the remedy: close apps versus delete files.",
        "Updates behave differently by layer, and understanding why removes frustration. Operating system updates arrive yearly or quarterly and change significant features. App updates ship weekly or monthly with smaller fixes. Security patches land whenever vulnerabilities appear. Each layer updates on its own schedule because each evolves independently, exactly like a house where paint changes monthly while the roof changes yearly. Letting each layer update automatically keeps the whole structure sound without your attention.",
        "Battery longevity depends less on luck than on habits, and the science is settled enough for simple rules. Batteries degrade fastest at temperature extremes and at chronic extremes of charge. Avoid leaving devices baking in hot cars, remove thick cases during fast charging if heat builds, and if a device lives plugged in constantly, enable whatever optimized-charging feature the manufacturer provides. Expect roughly five hundred full cycles before noticeable decline; treating batteries gently stretches that count substantially.",
        "Backups are the difference between an inconvenience and a catastrophe, and the professional standard is the 3-2-1 rule adapted for normal life: keep important data in at least two places, on the device plus one independent copy, ideally cloud-synced automatically. Phones sync photos to cloud services with a few taps; computers can back up to external drives on schedule. Test recovery once, restore one file deliberately, because an untested backup is a hope, not a backup.",
        "Choosing when to replace a device becomes rational with a simple framework: replace when it no longer runs supported software, when repair costs approach half of replacement, or when slowness genuinely blocks your work after cleanup attempts. Before buying, try cheaper revival first: free up storage, disable startup bloat, factory-reset as a last resort, which astonishingly often restores years of perceived speed by clearing accumulated cruft rather than any hardware fault.",
        "Develop basic hardware literacy sufficient to describe problems and evaluate help. Learn what ports your devices have, what a charging cable's wattage means, why adapters sometimes cause flakiness, and how to restart each device properly. When seeking repair, describe symptoms factually, what happened, when, what changed recently, rather than diagnoses, and get written estimates before approving work. Half of successful technology ownership is simply communicating clearly about it.",
    ]),
    ("The Cloud Without the Fog", [
        "The cloud is not a place in the sky; it is other people's computers that you rent instead of own. When your photos live 'in the cloud,' they sit on hard drives in warehouse-scale buildings operated by companies whose entire business is storing and protecting data reliably. That is the whole trick. Everything else, sync, sharing, backups, streaming, is scheduling and plumbing arranged among those machines and yours.",
        "Cloud storage solves several ancient problems at once. Files follow you across devices because a central copy exists that each gadget mirrors. Sharing stops involving attachments; you send links pointing at the same central file. Backups happen automatically because the central copy exists independent of any single device's fate. And capacity grows by subscription instead of hardware shopping. For most people, one reputable cloud storage service covering photos, documents, and essential files replaces an entire shelf of anxieties.",
        "Sync deserves careful understanding because it faithfully reproduces everything, including mistakes. Delete a file locally and sync deletes it centrally too, which surprises people who believed the cloud was a backup. The distinction matters: syncing keeps copies identical across locations, while backing up preserves versions over time. Robust setups do both, sync for convenience and versioned backup for disaster, since ransomware and accidental mass-deletion happily replicate through pure sync.",
        "Costs follow predictable tiers worth planning rather than discovering. Free allowances suit light users but fill quickly under photo accumulation, and photos are where most households collide with storage limits. Paid plans costing a few dollars monthly unlock terabytes and family sharing. Compare honestly against alternatives: one lost laptop containing irreplaceable photos costs more than a decade of cloud subscriptions. The expense buys redundancy, accessibility, and offsite safety that physical drives cannot match alone.",
        "Privacy in the cloud means making deliberate choices rather than default ones. Reputable providers encrypt data both in transit and at rest, but most scan content for policy violations and some surface it for advertising context. Services offering end-to-end encryption make your data unreadable even to the provider, trading some convenience for maximal privacy. Match sensitivity to service: tax documents and medical records justify stricter arrangements than recipe collections. Reading a provider's actual data policy once beats assuming forever.",
        "Cloud outages and account lockouts are rare but real, which argues for exit strategies. Keep local copies of truly critical documents, know the account-recovery process before needing it, and understand how to bulk-export your data, every major service offers download tools. This is not distrust; it is the same logic as keeping house keys with a neighbor. Independence from any single provider, maintained cheaply, converts worst cases back into inconveniences.",
        "Beyond storage, recognize how deeply the cloud already structures daily life, because recognizing it builds judgment. Streaming music and video are cloud delivery; navigation apps compute routes remotely; collaboration documents live centrally by design; smart-home devices phone home continuously. None of this requires suspicion, only awareness: each convenience involves data traveling somewhere and someone else's computers involved. Users who grasp this read privacy prompts meaningfully, choose services consciously, and troubleshoot confidently when 'the internet' misbehaves, knowing which link in the chain likely failed.",
    ]),
    ("Using AI Tools Wisely", [
        "AI assistants now draft text, answer questions, summarize documents, write code, and generate images, and they do so fluently enough to feel authoritative. Fluency and accuracy are different properties, however, and the single most important fact about AI tools is that they can be confidently wrong. They predict plausible language rather than verify facts, which means every consequential output needs human checking. Treat AI like a brilliant, tireless intern: enormously useful, never unsupervised.",
        "Getting good results depends mostly on asking well. Vague prompts yield vague output; specific context yields specific value. Compare 'write about gardening' against 'write a friendly 300-word checklist for beginners planting their first tomato containers, mention drainage and sun, avoid jargon.' The second prompt specifies audience, length, format, and constraints, and returns something usable immediately. Provide examples of style you want, state what to avoid, and iterate conversationally when the first attempt misses.",
        "Match tasks to strengths. AI excels at transforming existing material: summarizing long documents, rewording awkward passages, translating tone between formal and casual, generating outlines from bullet notes, explaining concepts at adjustable depths. It struggles more with novel factual claims, current events past its training, precise arithmetic inside long text, and anything requiring your private context it cannot see. Steering work toward transformation rather than invention captures the value while dodging the weaknesses.",
        "Verification protocols separate responsible users from casualties. Any statistic, date, quotation, legal claim, or medical claim generated by AI must be confirmed against a real source before use or repetition. Ask the tool for its sources, then check that those sources exist and say what was claimed, since fabricated references occur regularly. For high-stakes decisions, health, money, law, AI provides orientation and questions for professionals, never conclusions to act on alone.",
        "Privacy requires reading what you feed these services. Text entered into consumer AI tools may be stored and reviewed, so never paste passwords, client confidences, unreleased business information, or other people's personal data into tools lacking explicit confidentiality guarantees. Many services offer enterprise or opt-out modes with stronger protections. The working rule: enter only information you would be comfortable seeing on a whiteboard in a public place.",
        "AI also serves as a learning accelerator when used for dialogue rather than answers. Ask it to quiz you, argue the opposing position, explain a concept three different ways, or assess your draft against criteria. This tutoring mode builds your capability, whereas answer-generation mode can quietly erode skills you skip practicing. Students and professionals alike benefit from a simple self-check: am I using this tool to think better, or to avoid thinking?",
        "Keep perspective on the pace of change. Specific features will look dated quickly; the durable skills this chapter teaches, precise prompting, skeptical verification, privacy awareness, task matching, transfer across every future model. People who develop these habits treat each new capability as another tool to evaluate rather than magic to trust or menace to fear. That evaluative stance, curious and unsentimental, is the lifelong confidence this whole book aims to build.",
    ]),
    ("Digital Organization That Lasts", [
        "Digital clutter differs from physical clutter in one crucial way: files cost nothing to store, so mess accumulates invisibly until finding anything takes minutes of scrolling. Digital organization therefore matters less for tidiness than for retrieval. The goal is not beautiful folders; it is finding any file within thirty seconds, months later, without memory strain. Systems achieving that goal can look however they like; systems failing it fail regardless of elegance.",
        "Start with naming conventions, the highest-leverage habit in all of digital organization. A filename should carry its meaning standalone: '2026-03-14_lease-agreement_apartment.pdf' identifies itself forever, while 'Document2_final_FINAL(3).pdf' identifies nothing. Dates first sort chronologically everywhere; short descriptors second make searches unnecessary. Apply the convention going forward without relitigating history, since renamed archives bring little joy, and within months new files dominate anyway.",
        "Folders work best shallow and few. Deep hierarchies, Documents/Money/2024/Taxes/Quarterly/Q2, force perfect classification decisions at save time and guarantee misfiling. Two or three levels suffice: broad category, then year or project. Beyond that, rely on search, which modern systems perform brilliantly across file contents, not just names. The strategic insight: folders answer 'where does this belong,' search answers 'where did this go.' Shallow folders plus good names plus search beats deep taxonomy consistently.",
        "Photos need their own strategy because volume overwhelms generic approaches. Cloud photo services with automatic upload handle most organization through dates, faces, and locations, so the human job narrows to occasional culling, deleting duplicates and blurry shots monthly, and albums reserved for genuinely meaningful collections. Screenshots deserve special policing; they multiply silently, and ninety percent become worthless within weeks. A recurring ten-minute purge keeps the library humane.",
        "Email rewards ruthless triage over heroic sorting. Unsubscribe aggressively from anything unread for a month, since filters cannot save mail you never wanted. Archive rather than delete anything potentially relevant, because search retrieves archived mail as easily as filed mail, making elaborate folder schemes optional luxury. Handle the four Ds on each touch: do it if under two minutes, delegate it, defer it with a calendar reminder, or delete it. An empty-ish inbox is maintenance, not achievement.",
        "Password managers, bookmark bars, and note apps deserve brief attention since they hold organizational weight too. Consolidate scattered notes into one searchable tool with tags rather than folders. Prune bookmarks seasonally; dead links accumulate like weeds. Export contact lists periodically. Small consolidations compound: fewer places to check means fewer places to lose things, and every consolidation simplifies all future retrievals.",
        "Institutionalize maintenance with a quarterly digital hour: clear downloads folder, delete unused apps, review permissions, check storage headroom, verify backups ran, prune subscriptions. Put it in the calendar like dentistry. Organization systems decay without scheduled renewal, not from design flaws but from entropy, and the quarterly hour is cheap insurance keeping years of small habits pointed at the same goal: a digital life that serves you instead of hiding things from you.",
    ]),
    ("Troubleshooting Like a Pro", [
        "Professional troubleshooting is not talent; it is method. Technicians resolve issues faster than amateurs mainly because they proceed systematically instead of trying random fixes until exhaustion. The method fits in one sentence: reproduce the problem, isolate variables, test one change at a time, observe results, repeat. Anyone willing to slow down and follow it can fix the majority of everyday technology problems without calling anyone.",
        "Begin every incident with the restart ritual, which resolves a startling fraction of complaints. Restarting clears memory leaks, resets stuck processes, and re-establishes connections, fixing transient states that accumulated during long uptimes. Restart the device first, then the router if networking is involved, then the specific misbehaving app. Only after fresh starts fail does diagnosis properly begin, and experienced users restart reflexively before worrying about anything exotic.",
        "Next, define the failure precisely, because vague problems resist solving. 'The internet is down' might mean no websites load, or one website fails, or video buffers, or Wi-Fi shows connected but nothing transmits. Each description implicates different causes. Write down exactly what you did, exactly what you expected, and exactly what happened instead. This discipline alone prevents hours of chasing ghosts, since half of troubleshooting is realizing the reported problem was never quite the real problem.",
        "Then bisect the chain of dependencies. Internet problems form a chain, the remote site, the broader internet, your router, your device's connection, the specific app. Test each link: does another device work on the same network, isolating device versus network; does the problematic device work on another network, isolating network versus destination; does another website load, isolating connectivity versus the specific service. Each test eliminates half the remaining possibilities, which is where 'bisecting' earns its name.",
        "Read error messages slowly; they are documentation, not decoration. Codes like 404 mean the specific page was not found while connectivity works; 403 means permission denial; certificate warnings suggest clock settings or interception rather than hackers. Searching exact error text online usually surfaces known fixes within minutes, because nearly every common error has a thousand predecessors. Amateurs dismiss dialogs; professionals screenshot them and paste them verbatim into search.",
        "Change log discipline turns debugging sessions into progress. Write down every change attempted and its result, one change at a time, reverting failed experiments before trying the next. Random simultaneous tinkering creates compound confusion where fixes cancel fixes, and the original problem disappears under new self-inflicted ones. The notebook rule sounds bureaucratic and pays for itself the first time a fix works, because you know exactly what worked.",
        "Know escalation boundaries honestly. Hardware damage, anything involving electricity beyond cables, data-loss situations, and security compromises deserve professional hands sooner rather than later, especially data loss, where continued experimentation destroys recoverable files. Before escalating, gather the facts a technician needs: symptoms timeline, error messages, recent changes, steps already tried. Arriving organized cuts diagnostic fees and signals that your descriptions are trustworthy, which technicians reward with better service.",
    ]),
    ("Choosing Technology Deliberately", [
        "Buying technology badly is rarely about picking wrong products; it is about skipping the decision before the purchase. Ads, reviews, launch cycles, and social pressure create urgency designed to bypass reflection. Deliberate choosing inverts this: clarify the actual need, match capability to need without surplus, decide budget beforehand, and buy on your timeline rather than marketing's. The result is gear that serves for years and purchases without regret.",
        "Start every purchase with a written job description: what will this do weekly, where will friction currently exist, what would success look like in six months? 'I need a laptop' becomes 'I need a machine handling browser research, document editing, and video calls simultaneously, lasting through workdays away from outlets, under 900 dollars.' Specific jobs reveal that mid-range products satisfy most real needs and that flagship premiums buy headroom most buyers never use.",
        "Understand specifications just deeply enough to decode marketing. Processor names indicate generation and tier more than raw power claims; RAM matters most for heavy multitasking, storage capacity matters for media libraries, battery capacity predicts endurance roughly. Reviews measuring real performance beat spec sheets every time, since manufacturers optimize numbers that benchmark well rather than experiences you will have. Learn the two or three measurements relevant to your job description and ignore the rest guilt-free.",
        "Timing purchases beats impulse mathematically. Products depreciate sharply after successor launches; last generation's excellent device frequently halves in price while serving identical needs. Major sale seasons concentrate discounts predictably. Refurbished units from manufacturers, tested, warrantied, indistinguishable in daily use, deliver remarkable value relative to new. Patience of mere weeks routinely saves hundreds, and the technology you buy today was impossible at any price five years ago, deflating urgency considerably.",
        "Ecosystem lock-in deserves conscious evaluation before accumulation. Devices from one family interconnect seamlessly but deepen switching costs annually; mixing brands preserves flexibility at compatibility friction. Neither stance is wrong, but drift into lock-in unaware produces the worst outcome: dependency discovered only when leaving costs careers or hundreds in replacements. Periodically ask whether current arrangements still serve you, or whether you serve vendor convenience.",
        "Total cost of ownership extends past price tags into subscriptions, accessories, repairs, and eventual disposal. A cheap printer with costly ink, a tablet requiring paid stylus and keyboard, a device whose battery replacement costs half its value, these arithmetic traps catch shoppers comparing only sticker prices. Estimate three-year costs including consumables before deciding, and factor repairability ratings, since devices you cannot fix become landfill on schedule rather than by accident.",
        "Apply the same deliberateness to declining technology, which matters as much as acquiring it. Cancel subscriptions providing value below cost, retire gadgets performing duties your phone absorbed, and donate functioning equipment gathering drawers. Technology inventories grow by default and shrink only deliberately. An annual audit, listing everything owned, noting last genuine use, releasing the rest, keeps your environment aligned with your actual life instead of your purchasing history.",
    ]),
    ("Confidence for Life: Staying Capable as Technology Changes", [
        "Every specific technology in this book will eventually fade, and that is the plan working correctly. Interfaces, apps, platforms, and products cycle constantly, but the underlying competencies, systems thinking, security habits, structured troubleshooting, deliberate choosing, transfer indefinitely. Lifelong technological confidence is not about keeping up with everything, an exhausting impossibility. It is about owning a stable core of skills plus a healthy relationship with change itself.",
        "That relationship begins with dropping the myth of natural talent. People who feel 'bad with technology' almost always lacked exposure, not aptitude, and the confident people around them simply accumulated safe experimentation hours earlier. Competence with technology correlates strongly with willingness to click and see what happens, backed by the knowledge that most actions reverse easily. Curiosity plus low fear beats any amount of native brilliance, and both are choices available at any age.",
        "Calibrated caution makes experimentation safe. Distinguish reversible actions, creating documents, adjusting settings, exploring menus, which invite fearless play, from irreversible ones, deleting data, confirming payments, publishing publicly, which deserve deliberate pauses. Learn each new environment's undo mechanisms first. With that map, exploration becomes low-stakes learning rather than risk, and the explorers quietly outlearn the cautious memorizers every single time.",
        "Adopt a filtering strategy for new developments, because attending to everything guarantees absorbing nothing. Ignore incremental noise, app redesigns, marginal upgrades, hype cycles, and investigate only changes touching your actual life: security requirements, tools relevant to your work, capabilities affecting people you care about. Trusted curators help enormously here; find one or two explainers matching your style and let them pre-digest the torrent. Depth on the relevant tenth beats shallow acquaintance with everything.",
        "Learn transferable questions rather than transferless answers. Whenever mastering a new tool, extract the generalizable layer: what problem does this solve, what pattern does it follow, how does it compare to what I knew? Voice assistants, cloud services, and AI chatbots all seemed alien at arrival yet each followed recognizable patterns, subscription models, sync architectures, conversational interfaces. Pattern collectors experience novelty as variation on themes; memorizers experience it as perpetual strangeness.",
        "Maintain humility balanced against confidence, since overconfidence causes the falls that frighten people back into helplessness. Confident users still verify before deleting, read prompts before clicking through, and admit confusion readily, to search engines, support forums, or younger relatives. Asking is a competence, not a confession. The people who age gracefully alongside technology are precisely those who never pretended to know everything and therefore kept learning everything.",
        "Close with perspective worth keeping. Technology exists to serve human purposes, communication, health, creation, connection, and you remain the judge of purposes. Tools that serve your goals deserve adoption; tools demanding your life deserve rejection, regardless of fashion. Confidence ultimately means holding that authority steadily: able to learn what helps, decline what harms, adapt when change arrives, and teach others, which is how capability spreads beyond you to everyone whose life your competence touches.",
    ]),
]
